---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const updated = "October 4, 2026";
const email = "Peter.H.Hagen@gmail.com";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy – Peter Hagen"
  description="Privacy policy for phizou.github.io and the personal automation tools operated by Peter Hagen."
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        This site is a personal portfolio operated by Peter Hagen. It has no accounts, no sign-in,
        no comment system, and no advertising. The short version: I don't collect anything about you.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>phizou.github.io</strong> is a static site. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics or
        tracking scripts, and has no way to identify you. Nothing you do here is recorded by me.
      </p>
      <p>Two third parties are nonetheless involved in serving the page, and both are worth naming:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>GitHub Pages</strong> hosts the site. Like any web host, GitHub receives your IP
          address and browser user-agent in the ordinary course of delivering the page and may retain
          it for security and abuse prevention. This is governed by
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub's Privacy Statement</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google Fonts</strong> serves the typefaces from <code>fonts.googleapis.com</code> and
          <code>fonts.gstatic.com</code>, so your browser makes a request to Google and Google receives
          your IP address. See
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Privacy Policy</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I don't have access to either party's logs, and I don't combine, buy, sell, or share any
        visitor data — because I don't hold any.
      </p>

      <h2>Project demos</h2>
      <p>
        Some pages link out to live demos of my own projects, hosted on third-party platforms. Those
        are separate applications under their hosts' own terms and privacy practices. Anything you
        type into a demo is handled by that demo, not by this site.
      </p>

      <h2>Personal automation tools</h2>
      <p>
        I build and run private automation for my own accounts — including a tool that publishes
        short videos to <strong>my own YouTube channel</strong>. If you've arrived here from a Google
        consent screen, this is the policy that covers it.
      </p>
      <p>These tools:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>are operated by me, for me, on my own data and my own accounts;</li>
        <li>authorize only against my own Google account, using the narrowest scopes that work —
          uploading videos to my channel and reading back their status to confirm the upload succeeded;</li>
        <li>are not offered as a product or service to anyone else, and have no other users;</li>
        <li>store their credentials locally on my own machine and transmit them to no one;</li>
        <li>do not request, receive, or process any other person's Google data.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No data from these tools is sold, rented, or shared with third parties. Credentials are
        revocable at any time from my
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google account permissions</a>
        page.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        This site isn't directed at children and knowingly collects no information from anyone,
        including children under 13.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the updated date above changes with it. There's no mailing list to
        notify, by design.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 740px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 56px 24px 72px;
  }
  .head {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 22px;
    margin-bottom: 34px;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink-4);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0 0 10px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: 1.9rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
  }
  .updated {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink-5);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .prose {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 28px;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 34px 0 12px;
  }
  .prose p { margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .prose ul {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  .prose li { margin-bottom: 7px; }
  .prose strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .prose a {
    color: var(--teal);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .prose a:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--teal); }
  .prose code {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 0.82em;
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1px 5px;
  }
</style>
